            <div xml:id="d776">

                <pb facs="0911" n="793" sameAs="frus:frus1928v02/pg793"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">884.6461 Tsana Dam/51: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Minister in
                        Ethiopia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Southard</hi>)</head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1928-10-29T15:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">October 29,
                                1928—3 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>18. We communicated contents of your telegram of October 17th, 11 a.m., to J. G.
                    White Engineering Corporation. They point out, by way of reply, that it would be
                    difficult, if not altogether impossible, because of purely engineering reasons
                    for the British Government to reply to King Tafari’s inquiry concerning terms
                    under which water will be used and paid for, unless engineering agreement is
                    previously reached between the British authorities on the one hand and the White
                    Corporation on the other—the latter representing His Majesty Ras Tafari’s
                    interests and the interests of American stockholders who would invest forty
                    million Ethiopian dollars in Ethiopia under the direction of His Majesty. White
                    Corporation believe that a conference as described in Department’s 13, October
                    1, 6 p.m., would, if held before the departure of the new British Minister for
                    Addis Ababa, conduce to the reaching of an agreement regarding the large sums
                    which <pb facs="0912" n="794" xml:id="pg794"/> the British would be required to
                    pay for water, from which sums would come returns to American capital as well as
                    the important revenues accruing to the Government of Ethiopia.</p>
                <p>Your bringing the foregoing to the attention of King Tafari would be appreciated
                    by the J. G. White Corporation.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Kellogg</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
